\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A prime period enlarges the harmonic space}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{p} be a prime divisor of the positive integer #{N}, and
take #{d=1} with #{k=\mathbb{F}_p}. On the cycle of
\ref{fdip-00OK}, #{(df)(e_t)=f(t+1)-f(t)}. The counting adjoint of
\ref{fdip-00OL} sends an edge cochain #{g} to
#{(d^\dagger g)(t)=g(t-1)-g(t)}. Therefore
##{(\Delta f)(t)=2f(t)-f(t-1)-f(t+1).}
If #{\Delta f=0}, the first difference is constant, so
#{f(t)=a+bt} with #{a=f(0)}. The label #{t} is well-defined on
#{\mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z}} because #{p} divides #{N}, and every
such function is harmonic: its first difference is constant, and
the adjoint kills a constant edge cochain. Since #{N\ge p\ge 2},
the function #{t} is not constant. The kernel has rank #{2}. A
closed vertex function has difference #{0}, so the degree-zero
cohomology is the constants and that Betti number is #{1}.}
